  "prompt_text": "The figure explains different states of light polarization and how they relate to chiral optical responses. On the left, light propagates along the z direction while its electric field traces different paths: horizontal and vertical linear polarization oscillate in a single plane, whereas left- and right-handed circular polarization form helices of opposite handedness around the propagation axis. This visually contrasts simple linear motion with rotating polarization states.\n\nOn the right, these polarization states are connected to two common chiroptical measurements in a sample-filled cuvette. In the upper scheme, labeled CD, left- and right-circularly polarized light pass through the sample, implying a comparison of how the material differently absorbs the two circular polarizations. In the lower scheme, labeled CPL, the sample emits light; the emitted signal is shown either as a wavy unpolarized-looking trace or as left- and right-handed circular components, indicating analysis of the handedness of luminescence produced by the material.\n\nOverall, the figure presents chirality in optics as a distinction between opposite circular polarizations, and shows that this distinction can be probed either in absorption (CD) or in emission (CPL).",
